# Mosquitto ACL — restricts what each authenticated credential (see
# passwd.example) can publish/subscribe to. Contains no secrets (just
# usernames and topic patterns), unlike passwd, so this file is committed
# directly rather than generated from a .example template.
#
# Tenant isolation: device-facing credentials are one per tenant (username
# == tenant slug, e.g. "whirlpool"), and the `pattern` rule below expands
# %u to the connecting username — so a device authenticated as "whirlpool"
# can only touch topics under iot/whirlpool/#, never
# iot/abc-manufacturing/# or any other tenant's namespace, even if it
# knows another tenant's exact site/device codes. This stops
# cross-tenant spoofing.
#
# What this does NOT do: stop one device from impersonating another
# device in the SAME tenant — that needs true per-device credentials,
# which static password-file auth can't provision dynamically as devices
# are added/removed. That's a real follow-up (a dynamic auth backend, e.g.
# mosquitto-go-auth backed by the Devices table) — see
# docs/mqtt/reliability.md.

# Bms.Ingestion: the backend worker that receives every tenant's
# telemetry/heartbeat/status/events/commands-ack, and publishes outbound
# commands/config/firmware to any device — legitimately needs the whole
# iot/# namespace, not just one tenant's.
user bms-ingestion
topic readwrite iot/#
topic readwrite bms/realtime/events
# Its own retained liveness status (heartbeat + Last Will).
topic write bms/ingestion/bms-ingestion/status

# Bms.Api: relays the internal realtime-bridge topic into SignalR (read),
# and publishes outbound commands/config/firmware pushes to devices —
# MqttDeviceCommandDispatcher (used by the Commands, Configuration, and
# Firmware tabs) shares this same connection. Never reads device topics
# directly or handles telemetry ingestion — that's bms-ingestion's job.
user bms-api-realtime-bridge
topic read bms/realtime/events
# Ingestion liveness — pauses device-offline checks while ingestion is down.
topic read bms/ingestion/+/status
topic write iot/+/+/+/commands
topic write iot/+/+/+/config
topic write iot/+/+/+/firmware

# Connector Service: publishes readings translated from devices that send
# their own format (raw TCP etc.), for any company — so it may WRITE device
# telemetry topics everywhere, and nothing else: it reads no topic and can't
# send commands.
user bms-connectors
topic write iot/+/+/+/telemetry

# Per-tenant device credentials (see passwd.example for how these are
# created) — %u is substituted with the connecting username.
pattern readwrite iot/%u/+/+/#
